WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.400
Dear fellow scholars, this is Two Minute Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:11.400
When I was a bachelor student and took on my first bigger undertaking in computer graphics in 2011

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.200
it was a research project for a feature length movie

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.400
where the goal was to be able to learn the brush strokes of an artist;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.000
you see the sample brush stroke here.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.600
And what it could do is change the silhouette of a digital 3D object to appear,

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.060
as if it were drawn with this style.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.680
This way, we could use an immense amount of perfectly modeled geometry, and make them look

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.000
as if they were drawn by an artist.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.100
The project was a combination of machine learning and computer graphics

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.000
and got me hooked on this topic for life.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.040
So this was about silhouettes but what about being able to change the lighting?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.660
To address this problem, this new work promises something that sounds like science fiction.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.700
The input is a painting which is thought of as a collection of brush strokes.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:06.460
First, the algorithm is trying to break down the image into these individual strokes.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.140
Here on the left, with (a), you see the painting itself;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.840
and (b), is the real collection of strokes that were used to create it.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.840
This is what the algorithm is trying to estimate it with.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.400
And this colorful image visualizes the difference between the two.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.920
The blue color denotes regions where these brush strokes are estimated well;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.200
and we can find more differences as we transition into the red colored regions.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.240
So, great, now that we have a bunch of these brush strokes, but what do we do with them?

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:45.560
Well let's add one more assumption into this system, which is: that the densely packed regions

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:52.440
are going to be more effected by the lighting effects, while the sparser regions will be less impacted.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:58.840
This way, we can make the painting change as if we were to move our imaginary light source around.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.600
No painting skills or manual labor required.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.700
Wonderful.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:09.220
But, some of the skeptical fellow scholars out there would immediately ask the question:

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:15.420
It looks great, but how do we know if this really is good enough to be used in practice?

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:22.000
The authors thought of that too, and asked an artist to create some of these views by hand;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.180
and what do you know, they are extremely good.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.480
Very close to the real deal, and all this comes for free.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.880
Insanity.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.740
Now, we noted that the input for this algorithm is just one image.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:41.720
So, what about a cheeky experiment where we would add, not a painting, but a photo;

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.760
and pretend that it is a painting, can it relight it properly?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.160
Well hold onto your papers and let's have a look.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.140
Here is the photo;

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.720
the break down of the brush strokes, if this were a painting;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.660
and, wow! here are the lighting effects.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.020
It worked! and if you enjoyed these results, and would like to see more,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.920
make sure to have a look at this beautiful paper in the video description.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:18.760
For instance here, you see a comparison against previous works, and it seems quite clear that

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.360
it smokes the competition, on a variety of test cases.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.440
And these papers they're comparing to are also quite recent.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:31.060
The pace of progress in computer graphics research is absolutely incredible;

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.820
more on that in a moment.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.100
Also, just look at the information density here.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.500
This tiny diagram shows you exactly where the light source positions are;

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.280
I remember looking at a paper on a similar topic that did not have this thing,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.580
and it made the entirety of the work a great deal more challenging to evaluate properly.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:57.420
This kind of attention to detail might seem like a small thing, but it makes all the difference for a great paper;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.240
which, this one is.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:04.720
The provided user study shows that these outputs can be generated within a matter of seconds,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.260
and reinforces our hunch that most people prefer the outputs of the new technique to the previous ones;

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.340
so much improvement in so little time.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:20.340
With this, we can now create digital lighting effects from a single image, for paintings;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.860
and even photographs in a matter of seconds.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.860
What a time to be alive!

